xnu: kern.sf_exitwait, what the threads of a process stuck in exit wait for

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT (this header, the two new files and the added lines; the patched
files keep their Apple Public Source Licence)

A process can stay in exit for good (ps ?E/?Es, ppid 1, no threads listed, task_for_pid
failing, its descriptors and windows still open: SF-AH item 25, VLC after GPU-heavy runs).
sample cannot attach to it and the M1 has no stackshot reader, so nothing tells which kernel
wait it is in. This adds a report, off by default and root only, nothing runs until asked:

  sysctl kern.sf_exitwait.report         every process in exit (P_LEXIT, not yet a zombie):
                                         the state of its exit (lflag, proc ref bits, open
                                         descriptor table, task active/halting/thread counts),
                                         and per thread: state, wait event, block hint, owner
                                         of the kernel mutex / rw lock it waits for, msleep
                                         message, name, time blocked, continuation and the
                                         kernel frame chain as unslid return addresses
  sysctl kern.sf_exitwait.log=1          the same report into the kernel log (dmesg)
  sysctl kern.sf_exitwait.pid=N          also process N in any state (-1: every process)
  sysctl kern.sf_exitwait.kernel=1       also kernel_task's threads (driver and GPU workers)
  sysctl kern.sf_exitwait.at_shutdown=1  (or boot-arg sf_exitwait_shutdown=1) the report on
                                         the console after proc_shutdown, before unmounting

Taking the report cannot wait on what a stuck process holds: the proc lists are walked under
proc_list_lock without proc references (a process past proc_refdrain takes none; one in exit is
already on zombproc), tasks are kept by task references, the global thread lists are taken
with a try-lock (EBUSY after ~100 ms), every thread is held by a reference, and every word of
another thread's stack, of a lock or of a thread name is read with ml_nofault_copy() within
that thread's kernel stack. A thread that runs meanwhile is flagged "moved". Nothing in any
process is changed. Wait events and wait queues are VM_KERNEL_UNSLIDE_OR_PERM, as stackshot.

Files: bsd/kern/sf_exitwait_bsd.c (selection, format, sysctls, shutdown hook) and
osfmk/kern/sf_exitwait.c (thread snapshots); one call in kern_shutdown.c; two conf/files lines.
Applies (fuzz 0) after ../m1-upl-private/kernel.patch on kernel 48/50's series.
UPSTREAM: no (SiliconForest diagnostic).

